// What the screens say about each weekly portion of Deuteronomy (Devarim). The layout of each entry is
// explained at the top of lessons-genesis.js. Verse numbers follow the Hebrew Bible (as Sefaria does);
// in chapters 13, 23 and 29 they differ from English Bibles.
window.TORAH_LESSONS = window.TORAH_LESSONS || {};
Object.assign(window.TORAH_LESSONS, {

  'Devarim': {
    meaning: 'Words',
    summary: 'Deuteronomy is Moses’ farewell: a series of speeches to the new generation on the plains of Moab, just before he dies. He begins by retelling the journey: appointing judges, the disaster of the scouts, the forty years of wandering, and the victories over the kings Sichon and Og. This portion is always read on the Shabbat before Tisha B’Av.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'The man of few words finds his voice', text: 'Moses once said, “I am not a man of words.” Now a whole book is called <i>Devarim</i>, “Words.” Forty years of leading turned a hesitant speaker into the Torah’s great teacher.', source: 'Exodus 4:10 · Devarim Rabbah 1:7' },
      { title: 'Small cases matter too', text: '“Hear the small and the great alike; don’t be afraid of anyone.” Rashi says a case about a penny deserves the same care as a case about a fortune.', source: 'Deuteronomy 1:17 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Criticize without humiliating', text: 'Moses begins his rebuke with only hints, naming places where the people went wrong without spelling it out. Rashi says he did it to protect their dignity. Honest criticism doesn’t have to shame.', source: 'Deuteronomy 1:1 · Rashi' },
      { title: '“How can I alone?”', text: '“How can I bear your troubles, your burdens and your quarrels by myself?” The first word in Hebrew is <i>Eicha</i>, “How,” the same word that opens Lamentations, read on Tisha B’Av.', source: 'Deuteronomy 1:12' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 1:1', headline: 'Moses’ harshest speech hides every sin inside a place name.', text: 'Deuteronomy opens with what looks like a dull list of places: “between Paran and Tofel and Lavan, and Chatzerot and Di-zahav.” Rashi, following the Sifrei, says each name is a coded rebuke. <i>Lavan</i>, “white,” recalls their sneering at the white manna; <i>Di-zahav</i>, “plenty of gold,” recalls the golden calf. Moses names the failures of forty years and spells out none of them, “out of respect for Israel.” Rashi adds that he waited until the last weeks of his life, as Jacob did, so he would not have to repeat it and they would not have to keep facing him ashamed.', try: 'If you need to tell someone they let you down, do it today in a way that names the problem and leaves their dignity whole: in private, once, without replaying every detail.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 1:17', headline: 'Moses claimed the hardest cases, and a later one stumped him.', text: 'Moses sets up courts and charges the judges: don’t fear any man, hear the small case like the great, “and the matter that is too hard for you, bring to me and I will hear it.” Rashi, from the Talmud (Sanhedrin 8a), hears a flicker of pride in “bring to me,” and says it cost him: when the daughters of Zelophehad brought their claim, the law escaped him and he had to ask God. The Sifrei adds a parallel. Samuel announced “I am the seer,” and then looked at Jesse’s eldest son and was sure he was God’s anointed.', try: 'Today, when someone asks you something at the edge of what you really know, say “I’m not sure, let me check” instead of answering with confidence.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 1:27', headline: 'They decided God hated them, because they had stopped loving Him.', text: 'Moses recalls the night after the spies’ report: “You grumbled in your tents and said, ‘Because the LORD hates us, He brought us out of Egypt.’” Rashi answers: “He loved you, but you hated Him,” and quotes a folk saying: what is in your heart about your friend, you imagine is in his heart about you. The Midrash he cites spells out their argument. A king gives his favorite son the irrigated field and the other son the field that waits for rain; God took us from the Nile to a land of rain, so He must not love us.', try: 'Think of someone you’re sure dislikes you. Ask honestly how you feel about them, and do one thing for them today that you would never do for an enemy.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 1:45', headline: 'They wept before God, and Sforno says they cried for themselves.', text: 'After the decree, the people swing around: “We have sinned; we will go up and fight.” God says don’t; they go anyway and are chased down “as bees do.” Then: “You returned and wept before the LORD, but the LORD did not listen to your voice.” Ramban says Moses mentions the weeping to their credit: they truly regretted it, but an oath had already been sworn. Sforno is harder. Their repentance came from fear of the punishment, like Cain’s, Saul’s and Eli’s, and regret of that kind cannot undo what the sin set in motion.', try: 'Think of an apology you owe. Before you make it, ask whether you are sorry for what it did to them or for what it is costing you, and make the apology about them.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 2:26', headline: 'God ordered war; Moses sent peace instead, and God made it law.', text: 'God tells Moses to provoke Sichon, king of Heshbon, to war (2:24). Instead Moses sends messengers “with words of peace.” Rashi says no one commanded it; Moses learned it from God Himself, who offered the Torah to Esau and Ishmael though He knew they would refuse, and who sent Moses to speak gently to Pharaoh when one bolt of lightning would have done. The Midrash (Devarim Rabbah 5:13) says God was pleased: “By your life, I will fulfill your decree.” And so the law reads: “When you approach a city to fight it, call to it for peace.”', try: 'In a fight you are entitled to win, make the first offer of peace today, in words, before you make your case.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 3:7', headline: 'After the second victory, they trashed the spoil they had craved.', text: 'After defeating Sichon, the Torah says of the cattle, “we took as plunder for ourselves,” <i>bazaznu</i>. After defeating Og it says almost the same word with different vowels, <i>bazonu</i>. Rashi, from the Sifrei, hears in it <i>bizayon</i>, contempt. The first time, every man grabbed what he could. By the second, they were so full that they tore up cattle and clothes and threw them away, keeping only silver and gold. The same kind of animals, the same people; only their appetite had changed. Abundance had taught them contempt for what they once fought to get.', try: 'Pick one thing you once badly wanted and now ignore. Use it today with real attention, or give it to someone who would still be glad of it.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 3:18', headline: 'The tribes already settled had to lead the fight for everyone else.', text: 'Reuben and Gad have their land east of the Jordan, and their families can stay there. But Moses tells them: “You shall cross over armed before your brothers, all the men of valor,” until the others have rest too. Rashi says they literally marched at the front, because they were the strongest. Then Moses tells Joshua, “The LORD your God is the One who fights for you.” If God does the fighting, why must everyone come? The Or HaChaim answers that God fought Sichon and Og for twelve tribes standing ready, and that was no promise for ten.', try: 'Think of a hard stretch you are already through that someone close to you is in now. Call them today and set a time to show up in person, not only with a message.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vaetchanan': {
    meaning: 'And I pleaded',
    summary: 'Moses tells how he begged God to let him enter the land, and God said no. He urges the people to keep the commandments and never forget what they saw at Sinai. He repeats the Ten Commandments and teaches the Shema: “Hear, O Israel: the LORD is our God, the LORD is One,” with the command to love God with all your heart, soul and might. It’s read on Shabbat Nachamu, the Shabbat of comfort after Tisha B’Av.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Hear, O Israel', text: '“<i>Shema Yisrael</i>: the LORD is our God, the LORD is One.” The most famous line in Judaism is about listening. It’s said morning and night, and as a person’s last words.', source: 'Deuteronomy 6:4' },
      { title: 'Love with all of you', text: '“Love the LORD your God with all your heart, all your soul and all your might.” The Mishnah says “all your heart” means with both your good and your bad impulses: love with all of you, flaws included.', source: 'Deuteronomy 6:5 · Mishnah Berakhot 9:5' },
      { title: 'Prayer isn’t only for getting yes', text: 'The Midrash says Moses prayed 515 times to enter the land, and God still said no. He kept praying anyway, and spent his last days teaching. Prayer isn’t only about getting what we ask for.', source: 'Deuteronomy 3:23–26 · Devarim Rabbah 11:10' },
      { title: 'Do what’s right and good', text: '“Do what is right and good in the eyes of the LORD.” Ramban says this means going beyond the letter of the law: no list of rules covers every situation, so be decent where no rule applies.', source: 'Deuteronomy 6:18 · Ramban' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 3:23', headline: 'The man with the most merit begged for a gift, not wages.', text: 'Moses tells the people how he pleaded to cross the Jordan: <i>va-etchanan</i>, “I pleaded for grace.” Rashi notes that the root means an unearned gift. The righteous could point to their good deeds, but they ask only for what they don’t deserve. The Midrash (Devarim Rabbah 11:10) counts the letters of <i>va-etchanan</i>: 515, the number of times Moses prayed. Forty years of leading, the Red Sea, Sinai, and when he wants something for himself he comes empty-handed. The answer is still no: “Enough! Do not speak to Me of this again.”', try: 'Ask for something you want today, from God or from a person, without listing what you have done to earn it. Notice how it feels to ask that way.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 4:9', headline: 'Ramban makes forgetting Sinai a sin, because faith rests on witnesses.', text: '“Only guard yourself and guard your soul carefully, lest you forget the things your eyes saw… and make them known to your children and your children’s children.” Ramban calls Rashi’s reading here “completely incorrect” and counts the verse as a commandment: never forget the day at Sinai. His reason is striking. If the Torah rested only on Moses’ word, a later prophet with a miracle could shake it. But a whole people saw and heard, and parents do not lie to their children to leave them an empty inheritance. The faith is only as strong as the telling.', try: 'Tell someone younger one thing you have seen with your own eyes that shaped what you believe. Tell it as a witness, not as a lecture.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 4:41', headline: 'Moses spent his last weeks on a law that couldn’t work yet.', text: '“Then Moses set apart three cities across the Jordan, toward the sunrise,” refuges for anyone who kills by accident. The Talmud (Makkot 10a) says he knew they would protect no one until Joshua named three more in Canaan, a land Moses would never enter. He did it anyway: “A mitzvah has come my way; I will do it.” One sage hears God in the word “sunrise”: you have made the sun shine for killers. And the next verses run straight from these cities into “This is the Torah,” so Rabbi Yochanan says Torah itself is a city of refuge.', try: 'Start one good thing today that will only pay off after you have moved on: a recommendation, a fund, a habit you set up for someone else.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 5:18', headline: 'A peasant doesn’t covet a princess, and that is the whole commandment.', text: 'The last of the Ten Commandments forbids a feeling, and Deuteronomy adds a second verb: “do not crave.” How can anyone be commanded not to want? Ibn Ezra notes that people are amazed by this, and answers with a parable. A sensible peasant who sees a beautiful princess does not desire her; he knows she is out of reach. Once a person truly knows that what God gave his neighbor is not his to have, it becomes as far from him as the princess. The main purpose of the commandments, Ibn Ezra says here, is “to perfect the heart.”', try: 'Name one thing you keep wanting that belongs to someone else: their job, their partner, their life. Each time the wanting comes today, say plainly to yourself, that is not mine, and turn to something that is.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 5:26', headline: 'God wished for their heart, and they never asked Him for it.', text: 'After Sinai the people promise to listen and obey. God answers with a sigh: <i>mi yiten</i>, “If only they had such a heart, to revere Me always.” The Talmud (Avodah Zarah 5a) says Moses later called them “ingrates, children of ingrates”: when God said “if only,” they should have answered, “You give it to us.” Ingrates, like those who sneered at the manna; children of ingrates, like Adam blaming “the woman You gave me.” They offered God their promises, when what they needed was to ask for help keeping them.', try: 'Name one change you keep promising yourself you will make. Today, instead of promising again, ask for help with it: pray for it in your own words, or ask one person to hold you to it.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 6:5', headline: 'Rabbi Akiva waited a lifetime to keep one line of the Shema.', text: '“Love the LORD your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your might.” Rabbi Akiva taught that “all your soul” means even if He takes your soul. The Talmud (Berakhot 61b) tells how Rome arrested him for teaching Torah. As they tore his flesh with iron combs, he recited the Shema. His students asked: even now? He said: all my life this verse troubled me; when would I be able to fulfill it? He drew out the word <i>echad</i>, “one,” until he died. Even the angels protested: this is Torah, and this its reward?', try: 'Say the Shema slowly tonight and stop at “with all your heart.” Ask yourself what you would honestly refuse to give up for what you believe, and sit with the answer.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 7:9', headline: 'Love lasts a thousand generations longer than fear, the Talmud says.', text: 'Moses says God keeps faith “with those who love Him and keep His commandments, to a thousand generations.” In the Ten Commandments the number was “thousands.” Rashi, from the Talmud (Sotah 31a), explains the difference: those who obey out of fear earn a thousand generations, those who serve out of love two thousand. On the Shema, Rashi had already said what service from fear is worth: a servant who obeys his master only out of fear walks away when the master troubles him too much. The commandments are the same. What changes is whether they survive the hard years.', try: 'Pick one obligation you keep mostly out of fear of what happens if you don’t: a call, a visit, a practice. Today find one real reason to love it, and do it for that reason.' },
    ],
  },

  'Eikev': {
    meaning: 'Because',
    summary: 'Moses promises blessings if the people keep the commandments, and warns them not to grow arrogant when they prosper in the land: “Don’t say, my own strength made me rich.” He reminds them of the manna, the golden calf, and the second tablets. He asks what God really wants of them. The portion includes the second paragraph of the Shema and the source of grace after meals.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Say thanks when you’re full', text: '“When you have eaten and are satisfied, bless the LORD your God for the good land He gave you.” This is the source of <i>Birkat HaMazon</i>, grace after meals. Gratitude belongs to the times we’re full, not only the times we’re hungry.', source: 'Deuteronomy 8:10' },
      { title: 'Don’t take all the credit', text: '“Beware of saying to yourself: My own power and the strength of my hand made me this wealth.” Success makes it easy to forget who and what helped along the way.', source: 'Deuteronomy 8:17–18' },
      { title: 'Not by bread alone', text: '“A person does not live by bread alone, but by everything that comes from the mouth of the LORD.” We need food, and we also need meaning.', source: 'Deuteronomy 8:3' },
      { title: 'A hundred blessings a day', text: '“What does the LORD your God ask of you?” Rabbi Meir read “what” (<i>mah</i>) as “a hundred” (<i>me’ah</i>): say a hundred blessings every day. A day full of thank-yous is what God asks.', source: 'Deuteronomy 10:12 · Menachot 43b' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 8:10', headline: 'God bends His own rule for Israel, because they bless after crumbs.', text: '“You will eat and be satisfied, and bless the LORD your God.” The Torah only requires grace after a filling meal. The Talmud (Berakhot 20b) imagines the angels challenging God: Your Torah says You show no favor, yet the priestly blessing says “May the LORD lift His face toward you.” God answers: how can I not favor them? I told them to bless when they are satisfied, and they are strict with themselves to bless after a piece of bread the size of an olive or an egg. Gratitude beyond what is owed draws favor beyond what is due.', try: 'Say a blessing, or a real thank-you, today after something too small to require one: a snack, a held door, a quick reply. Make it specific.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 8:16', headline: 'The Torah calls bread from heaven an affliction.', text: 'Moses reminds them of God “who fed you manna in the wilderness… in order to afflict you.” Food falling from the sky every morning, and he calls it suffering? The Talmud (Yoma 74b) records two answers. Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Asi disagree: one says a person with bread in his basket is not like one without, and manna never kept overnight, so every night they went to sleep with nothing for tomorrow. The other says eating what you can’t see is not like eating what you see; it could taste of anything, but it always looked like manna.', try: 'Notice one part of your life where you live day to day with no cushion: money, health, time, energy. Instead of only worrying about it, take one small step today to put something in the basket.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 9:20', headline: 'Moses’ prayer for his brother saved only half of Aaron’s sons.', text: 'Retelling the golden calf, Moses reveals something Exodus never said: “The LORD was very angry with Aaron, to destroy him, and I prayed for Aaron too at that time.” Rashi says God was angry because Aaron gave in to the people, and that “destroy” means his children, as the prophet says, “I destroyed his fruit above.” Moses’ prayer atoned for half: two sons, Nadab and Abihu, later died, and two, Eleazar and Ithamar, lived. Moses fought for the brother whose weakness had made the calf, and even his prayer did not undo everything. Half was what it saved.', try: 'Pray today, in your own words and by name, for someone whose mistake hurt you or others. Ask for what can still be saved, even if not everything can.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 10:2', headline: 'The holiest box in Israel held a pile of broken stone.', text: 'God tells Moses: “I will write on the tablets the words that were on the first tablets, which you broke, and you shall put them in the Ark.” The Talmud reads it as both: the new tablets and the pieces of the old ones lay together in the Ark (Menachot 99a). The first set was God’s own work; the second, Moses had to carve. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi draws the lesson (Berakhot 8b): honor an elder who has forgotten his learning through no fault of his own. What once held the words of God is not demoted because it broke.', try: 'Call or visit someone whose powers are fading: an aging teacher, a grandparent, a friend after illness. Ask about what they once knew best, and let them be the expert again for an hour.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 10:17', headline: 'Maimonides: a thousand good deeds cannot buy off one wrong.', text: 'Moses describes “the great, mighty and awesome God, who shows no favor and takes no bribe.” What bribe could anyone offer God? Maimonides answers in his commentary on Pirkei Avot: our good deeds. If a person did a thousand good things and one evil, God does not cancel the one for the thousand. He rewards every good deed and still holds him to the wrong. Ramban reads it more socially: God takes no bribe from the rich in their quarrels with the poor, which is why the next verse says He does justice for the orphan and the widow.', try: 'Think of a wrong you have been quietly balancing against all the good you do. Deal with it on its own today: apologize, repay or fix it, without mentioning your good side.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 11:14', headline: 'The Talmud tested two ways to live, and reported which one failed.', text: '“You shall gather in your grain, your wine and your oil.” Rabbi Yishmael says the verse is a correction: don’t take “study it day and night” literally; live in the way of the world, working and learning together. Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai protests: if a person plows, sows, reaps, threshes and winnows in their seasons, what becomes of Torah? Give yourself to it entirely, and others will do your work. Then the Talmud (Berakhot 35b) does something rare: it reports the results. Abaye says many lived like Rabbi Yishmael and succeeded; many lived like Rabbi Shimon and did not.', try: 'Pick one ideal you keep postponing until you can do it perfectly: study, prayer, exercise, a creative project. Fit a small, real piece of it into the life you actually have, today.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 11:22', headline: 'You can’t cling to fire, so cling to those who carry it.', text: '“To walk in all His ways and to cling to Him.” Rashi, from the Sifrei, asks the obvious question: how? He is “a consuming fire.” The answer: cling to scholars and the wise, and I will count it as clinging to Me. The Talmud (Ketubot 111b) tells where this comes from. Rabbi Yochanan was grieving over ordinary people who had never learned, and his student Rabbi Elazar found them a remedy: whoever gives his daughter in marriage to a scholar, does business for scholars or supports them is counted as attached to the Divine Presence itself.', try: 'Today, contact one person who is wiser or more learned than you and set a time to learn with them, or support their work in a concrete way.' },
    ],
  },

  'Re\'eh': {
    meaning: 'See',
    summary: '“See, I set before you today a blessing and a curse.” Moses tells the people to worship God in the one place God will choose, and to get rid of idol worship. He warns against false prophets, lists kosher and non-kosher animals, and commands tithes, canceling debts every seventh year, generosity to the poor, freeing servants, and the three pilgrimage festivals: Passover, Shavuot and Sukkot.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'The choice is yours', text: '“See, I set before you today a blessing and a curse.” Every day is a fresh choice. Maimonides says every person can become as righteous as Moses or as wicked as the worst king; no one decides that for you.', source: 'Deuteronomy 11:26–28 · Rambam, Laws of Repentance 5:2' },
      { title: 'Open your hand', text: '“Do not harden your heart or close your hand against your needy brother. Open your hand to him.” Generosity starts with not closing up.', source: 'Deuteronomy 15:7–8' },
      { title: 'Celebrate with everyone', text: 'On the festivals, rejoice with your family and also “the Levite, the stranger, the orphan and the widow.” Maimonides says a celebration that shuts out the poor isn’t the joy of a mitzvah; it’s just the joy of the stomach.', source: 'Deuteronomy 16:14 · Rambam, Laws of Festivals 6:18' },
      { title: 'The need never ends', text: '“There will never stop being poor people in the land; that’s why I command you to open your hand.” The need never runs out, so neither should the giving.', source: 'Deuteronomy 15:11' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 11:26', headline: 'Sforno: Israel was never offered an average life.', text: '“See, I set before you today a blessing and a curse.” The first word is singular, <i>re’eh</i>, “you, look,” while the rest speaks to everyone, as if each person must see for himself a choice the whole people faces. Sforno reads that first word as a warning: look, your lot will not be middling, the way it usually is for other nations. The blessing is one extreme, success beyond what is enough; the curse is the other, a lack in which even enough is out of reach. Both are set before you, to reach by what you choose.', try: 'Name one area of your life you have kept deliberately lukewarm, neither committed nor quitting. Today make one move that commits you.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 12:23', headline: 'If blood needs “be strong,” imagine what the things we crave need.', text: 'Moses permits eating meat away from the Temple, with one condition: “Only be strong not to eat the blood, for the blood is the life.” Why “be strong” about something most people find revolting? Rashi brings two answers from the Sifrei. Rabbi Yehuda: they were used to eating blood and craved it. Ben Azzai disagrees: no one longs for blood, and still the Torah says “be strong.” It is teaching how hard we must work at the commandments we actually want to break. If the easiest prohibition needs strength, how much more the ones that pull at us.', try: 'Pick the rule you keep for yourself that you break most often: about food, a screen, money, your temper. Today treat it as needing real strength, and decide in advance exactly where you will hold the line.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 13:18', headline: 'Even a commanded act of violence, Or HaChaim warns, breeds cruelty.', text: 'The law of the condemned city is among the Torah’s most terrible: if a whole town turns to idols, it is destroyed. Then a promise: “and He will give you mercy, and have mercy on you.” Why must God give mercy to the ones who obeyed? The Or HaChaim answers that killing, even when commanded, plants cruelty in the heart of the one who does it, and God promises to put compassion back. The Talmud (Sanhedrin 71a) records the view that such a city never was and never will be; Rabbi Eliezer says a single mezuzah would save it.', try: 'Think of a time you were harsh because you were sure you were right. Today do one deliberately gentle thing toward that person, or someone like them, to put the mercy back.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 14:1', headline: 'A law against gashing for the dead became a law against splitting.', text: '“You are children of the LORD your God; do not gash yourselves, and make no bald spot for the dead.” Rashi explains: you are God’s children, so it is fitting for you to be beautiful, not cut and torn. Ramban rejects that reason: a law first given to the priests now goes to everyone, since the whole people is holy. Then the Talmud (Yevamot 13b) hears a second meaning in the odd form <i>lo titgodedu</i>: do not split into factions, <i>agudot</i>. Grief and division tear at the same body, and the children of one Father should do neither to themselves.', try: 'Think of a split in your family, community or circle of friends where you have quietly taken a side. Today do one thing that crosses the line: a call, an invitation, a kind word to the other camp.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 14:22', headline: 'Tithing is the one place the Talmud lets you test God.', text: '“You shall surely tithe,” <i>aser te’aser</i>. The Talmud (Taanit 9a) tells of Rabbi Yochanan meeting the young son of Resh Lakish and asking for the verse he learned that day. The boy recites this one and asks what the doubled word means. Tithe, <i>aser</i>, so that you will become rich, <i>titasher</i>. How do you know? Go and test it. But we may not test God! Rabbi Yochanan answers: nowhere else, except here, for the prophet says of tithes, “Test Me in this.” The boy shrugs: had I reached that verse, I wouldn’t have needed you.', try: 'Set aside a tenth of something this week, money or time, before you spend the rest, and give it away. Then watch honestly what it does to how you hold the other nine tenths.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 15:8', headline: 'Hillel once ran three miles in front of a poor man’s horse.', text: '“Open your hand to him, and lend him enough for his need, whatever he lacks.” Rashi, from the Sifrei, reads every phrase. If he won’t take a gift, make it a loan. “Enough for his need”: you needn’t make him rich. But “whatever he lacks” means what this person lacks: a man born to wealth who has lost it may need even a horse to ride and a servant to run before him. The Talmud (Ketubot 67b) says Hillel bought exactly that for a poor man of good family, and once, finding no servant, ran before him three miles himself.', try: 'When you help someone this week, ask what they actually lack, including their dignity, not what you think a person in their position should need, and give that.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 16:11', headline: 'Maimonides: a holiday feast without the poor is only a full belly.', text: '“Rejoice before the LORD your God: you, your son and your daughter, your servant and your maid, and the Levite, the stranger, the orphan and the widow.” Rashi, from the Midrash, counts: four are yours and four are Mine. If you gladden Mine, I will gladden yours. Maimonides turns it into law. On a festival you must feed the stranger, the orphan and the widow with the other poor. Whoever locks his courtyard gates and eats and drinks with his wife and children alone is not rejoicing in a mitzvah, “but in his belly.”', try: 'Before the next Shabbat or holiday meal you host or attend, invite one person who would otherwise eat alone, or send food to someone who has less.' },
    ],
  },

  'Shoftim': {
    meaning: 'Judges',
    summary: 'Appoint honest judges in every town, and pursue justice. Moses sets out the roles of courts, kings, priests and prophets: a king must not pile up horses, wives or gold, and must write his own Torah scroll. The portion includes the cities of refuge, a ban on moving boundary markers, rules of war (exemptions for the newly married and the frightened, and not cutting down fruit trees), and what to do about an unsolved murder.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Justice, justice', text: '“<i>Tzedek, tzedek tirdof</i>: justice, justice you shall pursue.” Why say it twice? Commentators say: pursue justice, and pursue it justly. The way you get there has to be fair too.', source: 'Deuteronomy 16:20' },
      { title: 'No one is above the law', text: 'The king must write his own copy of the Torah, keep it with him and read it all his life, “so that his heart won’t be lifted above his brothers.” Power needs limits.', source: 'Deuteronomy 17:18–20' },
      { title: 'Don’t destroy for nothing', text: 'Even in war, don’t cut down fruit trees: “Is the tree of the field a person, that it should be besieged by you?” The rabbis grew this into <i>bal tashchit</i>: don’t waste or destroy anything needlessly.', source: 'Deuteronomy 20:19' },
      { title: 'Communities are responsible', text: 'When a murdered stranger is found, the elders of the nearest town must declare, “Our hands did not shed this blood.” Rashi: did we send him off without food or someone to walk with him? A town answers for how it treats travelers.', source: 'Deuteronomy 21:7 · Rashi' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 16:20', headline: 'The Torah says “justice” twice, and one of them means compromise.', text: '“Justice, justice you shall pursue.” Why say it twice? A teaching in the Talmud (Sanhedrin 32b) answers: one for strict judgment and one for compromise. It gives a picture. Two boats meet on a narrow river; if both insist on passing, both sink, and if one waits, both get through. So too two camels on the steep ascent of Beit Choron. The rabbis argued fiercely about it (Sanhedrin 6b). One sage called brokering a compromise a sin against the law; Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korcha called it a mitzvah, because only compromise lets justice and peace live together.', try: 'In a standoff you are in right now, at work or at home, offer to go second through the narrow place: name one thing you will give up so that you both get through.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 17:17', headline: 'Solomon knew the rule’s reason, and that is what ruined him.', text: 'The king “shall not take many wives, so that his heart does not turn away,” nor many horses, lest he send the people back to Egypt for them. These are among the few laws whose reason the Torah states. The Talmud (Sanhedrin 21b) says that is exactly why it rarely does. Solomon, the wisest of men, read the reasons and reasoned past them: I will take many, and my heart won’t turn; I will keep many horses, and not go back to Egypt. Then: “When Solomon was old, his wives turned his heart.” And his chariots came from Egypt.', try: 'Name one rule you keep for yourself that you have recently argued yourself out of because you understand it so well. Keep it today exactly, with no exception.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 18:4', headline: 'The Torah set no amount, so the rabbis graded the giver’s eye.', text: '“The first of your grain, your wine and your oil, and the first of your sheep’s shearing, you shall give him.” The priests own no land and live on what the people hand them. But how much is “the first”? The Torah never says; Shmuel teaches that one grain of wheat fulfills the law (Chullin 137b). The rabbis set measures anyway, and named them for the eye: a generous eye gives a fortieth, an average eye a fiftieth, a stingy eye a sixtieth. The law sets a floor; the room above it shows what kind of eye you have.', try: 'Next time you give, a tip, a donation, your time, notice the minimum that would be acceptable. Then give at the level of a generous eye, on purpose.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 18:13', headline: 'An astrologer foresaw a man’s death; a quiet kindness cancelled it.', text: 'After banning diviners, the Torah says: “Be wholehearted with the LORD your God.” Rashi: walk with Him simply, don’t probe the future, and accept whatever comes. The Talmud (Shabbat 156b) argues that Israel has no fixed star: Shmuel sat with the astrologer Ablet, who pointed at a man heading to the lake: a snake will bite him, he won’t come back. He came back; in his bundle lay a snake cut in two. That day, he explained, one man in his group had no bread to share, so he pretended to collect from him, to spare his shame.', try: 'Skip the forecast you usually check to set your mood: the horoscope, the market, the worst-case scenario. Instead, do one quiet kindness today that spares someone embarrassment.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 19:5', headline: 'An exiled student’s teacher had to go into exile with him.', text: 'A man is chopping wood; the axe head flies off and kills his neighbor. “He shall flee to one of these cities and live.” The rabbis lean on that last word. <i>Va-chai</i>, and live: give him what he needs to have a life there (Makkot 10a). So if the killer is a student, his teacher is exiled with him, and if a teacher, his whole academy. Maimonides, citing this verse, explains why: for a person who seeks wisdom, life without study is like death. And Rabbi Zeira adds a warning for teachers: be careful whom you teach.', try: 'Think of someone who learns from you, at work or at home, who is going through a hard time. Reach out to them today rather than waiting for them to come to you.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 20:8', headline: 'The Torah gave sinners a cover story for leaving the battle.', text: 'Before battle the officers announce exemptions: whoever has built a house, planted a vineyard or is engaged to marry, go home. Then: “Who is the man who is afraid and fainthearted? Let him go home.” Rabbi Akiva takes it literally: he can’t face a drawn sword. Rabbi Yosei the Galilean says it is the man afraid of the sins he has committed. That is why the other exemptions come first, Rashi explains from the Talmud (Sotah 44a): so anyone seeing a man walk home would say, maybe he built a house. The law protects his secret along with his life.', try: 'If someone near you backs out of something today, don’t ask why. Give them an easy, honorable way out, and keep any guesses to yourself.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 21:7', headline: 'A town’s elders swore they never sent a stranger off hungry.', text: 'A body is found in a field and no one knows the killer. The elders of the nearest town break a calf’s neck in a wild valley, wash their hands and declare: “Our hands did not shed this blood.” Rashi asks: would anyone suspect the elders of murder? They are swearing that he never came to them and left without food, or without someone to walk him out. The Talmud (Sotah 46b) adds Rabbi Meir’s view: people may be compelled to escort a traveler, because its reward has no measure. A town answers for whoever passes through it alone.', try: 'When someone leaves your home or office today, don’t say goodbye from your chair. Walk them out, to the door, the elevator or the car, and make sure they have what they need for the road.' },
    ],
  },

  'Ki Teitzei': {
    meaning: 'When you go out',
    summary: 'This portion has more commandments than any other: 74, by Maimonides’ count. They cover family law, returning lost property, helping a fallen animal, putting a railing on your roof, sending away a mother bird before taking her eggs, honest weights, paying workers the same day, kindness to the stranger, the orphan and the widow, and remembering what Amalek did.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Don’t look away', text: 'If you see a neighbor’s animal or belongings lost, “you may not hide yourself”: return them. Not looking away when someone needs help is itself a mitzvah.', source: 'Deuteronomy 22:1–3' },
      { title: 'Build a railing', text: '“When you build a new house, put a railing around your roof, so you don’t bring blood on your house if someone falls.” We’re responsible for preventing harm, not just for not causing it.', source: 'Deuteronomy 22:8' },
      { title: 'Pay people on time', text: '“Pay him his wages the same day, before sunset, for he is poor and is counting on it.” Late pay isn’t a small thing for someone who needs it to live.', source: 'Deuteronomy 24:15' },
      { title: 'Remember Amalek', text: 'Amalek attacked the weak and the tired at the back of the line. “Remember what Amalek did to you.” Cruelty to the vulnerable must be remembered, and resisted.', source: 'Deuteronomy 25:17–19' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 21:11', headline: 'Rashi reads three laws in a row as one man’s ruined life.', text: 'A soldier sees a beautiful captive and wants her. The Torah permits it, after a month of mourning for her father and mother. Rashi says frankly it is a concession to desire: forbid it and he would take her anyway. Then he reads the next laws as their story: a man with two wives, “one loved and one hated,” and then a stubborn, rebellious son. Marry her this way, and you will come to hate her and raise a son who rebels. One view in the Talmud (Sanhedrin 71a) holds that such a son never was and never will be.', try: 'Name one thing you want right now that you already suspect you will regret. Before acting on it, wait, the way the Torah makes the soldier wait a month, and see what is left of the wanting.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 22:7', headline: 'A boy died obeying two commandments that promised him long life.', text: 'Send away the mother bird before taking the chicks, “so that it may go well with you and you will live long.” Only one other law carries this same double promise: honoring parents. In the Talmud (Kiddushin 39b) a father sends his son up to fetch chicks. The boy sends off the mother, takes the young, and falls to his death. Some say Elisha ben Abuya saw this and lost his faith. His grandson, Rabbi Yaakov, answered: long life means in the world that is wholly long. Had Elisha read it so, says Rav Yosef, he would not have sinned.', try: 'Think of something right you keep doing that has never paid off, or has cost you. Decide whether you would still do it if it never paid off in this life, and then do it once more today.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 22:8', headline: 'He may deserve to fall, but not from your roof.', text: '“When you build a new house, make a railing for your roof, so that you do not bring blood on your house if the faller falls from it.” Rashi notices the odd phrase “the faller,” as if the man already deserved to fall. Even so, the Sifrei says, his death should not come through you: good comes about through the good, and harm through the guilty. His guilt doesn’t excuse your missing railing. Rashi also links it to the bird’s nest just before: one good deed brings another, and whoever spared the mother bird comes to build a house.', try: 'Walk through your home, car or workplace today looking for the one hazard you have been meaning to fix: a loose rail, a dead smoke alarm, a worn tire. Fix it today.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 23:9', headline: 'The Torah forgave Egypt’s drownings sooner than Moab’s seduction.', text: '“Do not abhor an Egyptian, for you were a stranger in his land.” Rashi admits Egypt threw Israel’s sons into the Nile; still, it hosted them in a famine, and its grandchildren may join the people. Edom met them with a sword and is treated the same. But Ammon and Moab, who hired Bilaam against them and drew them into sin, are shut out forever. Rashi draws the lesson from the Sifrei: whoever makes a person sin does him worse than whoever kills him. A killer takes this world from him; the seducer takes this world and the next.', try: 'Think of a habit or a group you have drawn someone into that is bad for them: a vice, a cynicism, a grudge. Today undo your part: tell them so, or stop pulling them in.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 24:1', headline: 'The Torah permits divorce; the Talmud says the altar weeps over it.', text: 'The Torah’s law of divorce is almost an aside: if a man finds “something unseemly” in his wife, he writes her a bill of divorce. The Mishnah (Gittin 9:10) records a famous argument over those words. Beit Shammai: only for real infidelity. Beit Hillel: even if she burned his dinner. Rabbi Akiva: even if he found someone more beautiful. Divorce stays permitted, and then the Talmud (Gittin 90b) adds its own verdict. Rabbi Elazar says that whoever divorces his first wife, even the altar sheds tears over him, as the prophet says of “the wife of your youth.”', try: 'Think of the relationship in your life, family, friendship or love, that you have come closest to giving up on. Today do one thing for it that the part of you that wants to stay would do.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 24:5', headline: 'Rashi corrects a translation: the first year is for her joy.', text: 'A newly married man is free of army service for a year: “he shall be free for his house one year, and gladden his wife.” Rashi stops on one vowel. The verb is <i>ve-simach</i>, he shall make her glad, not <i>ve-samach</i>, he shall be glad. Whoever translates it into Aramaic as “he shall rejoice with his wife,” Rashi says, is mistaken. The year is not a honeymoon for the husband; it is a duty to her. The nation puts off its wars so that one new wife can be happy, and the soldier’s whole assignment that year is her.', try: 'Do one thing today whose only purpose is someone else’s happiness, a parent, a friend, someone you love, chosen by what delights them rather than by what you would enjoy together.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 25:18', headline: 'Amalek’s crime, Rashi says, was cooling a boiling bath.', text: '“Remember what Amalek did to you on the way… how he met you, <i>karcha</i>, and struck the stragglers.” Rashi reads <i>karcha</i> as “cooled you.” After the Exodus every nation was afraid to touch Israel. Amalek was like a reckless man who jumps into a scalding bath no one dared enter. He gets burned, but now everyone thinks the water is cool. Rashi adds that Amalek picked off the weak at the back, the exhausted and thirsty, “and he did not fear God.” The first cynic doesn’t win the fight; he makes it thinkable for everyone else.', try: 'Notice the next time you feel the urge to deflate someone’s excitement or awe with a joke or a shrug. Today, hold back, and let the thing stay hot.' },
    ],
  },

  'Ki Tavo': {
    meaning: 'When you come in',
    summary: 'When the people enter the land, they are to bring their first fruits to the Temple and tell the story of their history: “My father was a wandering Aramean.” They’re to set up stones with the Torah written on them, and the tribes will call out blessings and curses on two mountains. Moses describes the blessings for keeping the covenant and long, frightening warnings for breaking it, and says: “Until today, God hadn’t given you a heart to understand.”',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Tell your story with thanks', text: 'Bringing his first fruits, a farmer tells the whole history: a wandering ancestor, slavery, rescue, and now this harvest. Gratitude is fuller when you remember where you came from. These words became the heart of the Passover Haggadah.', source: 'Deuteronomy 26:5–10' },
      { title: 'Celebrate with others', text: '“Rejoice in all the good the LORD your God has given you, you and the Levite and the stranger among you.” Good fortune is meant to be shared.', source: 'Deuteronomy 26:11' },
      { title: 'Each generation signs up', text: 'In Moab, Moses makes a covenant with the new generation, “besides the covenant made at Sinai.” Every generation has to choose the relationship for itself.', source: 'Deuteronomy 28:69' },
      { title: 'Understanding takes time', text: '“Until this day, the LORD hasn’t given you a heart to know, eyes to see, and ears to hear.” Rashi: only after forty years does a student truly understand his teacher. Some understanding only comes with time.', source: 'Deuteronomy 29:3 · Rashi' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 26:5', headline: 'So no farmer would be shamed, even fluent readers were prompted.', text: 'The farmer sets his basket of first fruits before the altar and recites a set speech aloud: <i>Arami oved avi</i>. Rashi reads it “an Aramean tried to destroy my father”: Laban, chasing Jacob. Gratitude begins with remembering how close you came to not existing. But the Hebrew was hard. The Mishnah (Bikkurim 3:7) says that at first those who could read recited it, and the rest repeated after a prompter, until the ones who couldn’t read stopped coming. So the sages ruled that everyone, fluent or not, would repeat after the prompter. A ritual that shames anyone has failed.', try: 'Find one place in your life, a table, a group chat, a synagogue, where someone quietly feels behind. Change one thing about how it runs today so they don’t have to stand out.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 26:15', headline: 'After giving to the poor, the farmer tells God: your turn.', text: 'In the third year the farmer gives his tithe to the Levite, the stranger, the orphan and the widow, then declares: “I have done all that You commanded me. Look down from Your holy dwelling and bless Your people.” Rashi spells out the nerve of it: “We have done what You decreed on us; now You do what is on You to do.” Elsewhere Rashi notes that when Scripture says God “looks down,” it is always for harm, except here, because giving to the poor is so powerful that it turns anger into mercy.', try: 'Give something today to someone who can’t pay you back, a real amount, not spare change. Then, in your own words, ask for the thing you need most, as someone who has done their part.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 26:17', headline: 'The Talmud says God wears tefillin, and they praise Israel.', text: '“You have affirmed the LORD today… and the LORD has affirmed you today.” Rashi admits he can find no proof for what the rare verb means; he guesses it means singling out: you chose Him from all gods, He chose you from all nations. The Talmud (Berakhot 6a) goes further. Our tefillin declare “Hear, O Israel, the LORD is one.” God’s tefillin, it says, hold the answering verse: “Who is like Your people Israel, one nation on earth?” You made Me unique in the world, God says, so I will make you unique. Devotion runs both ways.', try: 'Write the one sentence you would bind on your arm about someone you love, what makes them irreplaceable to you. Today say it to them directly.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 27:9', headline: 'The Talmud says those who study alone grow foolish.', text: 'Moses and the priests say: <i>haskeit</i> and hear, O Israel. The rare word seems to mean “be silent,” but the Talmud (Berakhot 63b) hears in it <i>asu kitot</i>, “form groups,” for Torah is acquired only with others. Rabbi Yosei son of Rabbi Chanina is harsher: a sword hangs over scholars who sit alone and study, and they grow foolish. Another reading splits it: first be silent and take it in, then cut into it and argue. Rava agrees: learn first, analyze after. The ideal is not the lone genius but someone who can be argued with.', try: 'Take a belief you hold strongly, about politics, faith or a person, and put it today to someone who will push back. Hear them out fully first, then argue.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 27:26', headline: 'The final curse can fall on a perfectly righteous scholar.', text: 'At Mount Ebal the Levites call out twelve curses; the people answer “Amen.” The last is for whoever “does not uphold the words of this Torah.” The Jerusalem Talmud asks, as the Ramban quotes it: is there a Torah that falls down? One answer: even if a person learned and taught, kept and did, but had the means to strengthen Torah for others and didn’t, he is under the curse. Another: it is the synagogue official who fails to lift the scroll so everyone can see the writing. Private piety isn’t enough if you could have held it up.', try: 'Name something good you love, a teacher, a school, a cause, a tradition, that is struggling and that you could help hold up. Today give it money, time or a phone call.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 28:47', headline: 'Amid the worst curses in the Torah, the stated cause is joylessness.', text: 'Fifty-four verses of siege, disease and exile, so harsh that many read them in an undertone. In the middle comes the reason: “because you did not serve the LORD your God with joy and a glad heart, when you had plenty of everything.” Maimonides makes it law: the joy of a mitzvah, out of love for God, is “a great service,” and whoever holds back from it deserves punishment. Whoever guards his dignity at such moments is a sinner and a fool. True greatness is to rejoice before God, like King David leaping and dancing before the Ark.', try: 'Pick one duty you do on autopilot, a prayer, a call to your parents, a chore for someone, and do it today with your whole self in it, even if you look a little foolish.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 29:3', headline: 'Forty years of miracles, and a complaint is what finally pleased Moses.', text: 'Moses recalls the plagues, signs and wonders, “but the LORD has not given you a heart to know, eyes to see, or ears to hear, until this day.” Why this day? Rashi has heard a story. When Moses handed the Torah scroll to his own tribe, Levi, the whole people came to protest: we also stood at Sinai; tomorrow they will say it was given only to them! Moses rejoiced. Only now did he know they truly longed for God. Rashi adds a second reading, from the Talmud: no one grasps his teacher’s mind for forty years.', try: 'Ask yourself what you would protest if someone tried to take it from you. That is what you actually love; give it a real hour today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Nitzavim': {
    meaning: 'Standing',
    summary: 'On his last day, Moses gathers everyone (leaders, children, women, strangers, woodcutters and water-carriers) to enter the covenant, including “those who are not here with us today.” He promises that even after exile, if the people return to God with all their heart, God will bring them back. The Torah “is not in heaven”; it’s “very close to you.” And: “I set before you life and death… choose life.”',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'A promise across time', text: 'Moses makes the covenant “with those standing here with us today, and with those who are not here with us today.” Rashi: the generations not yet born. Every future Jew was included.', source: 'Deuteronomy 29:13–14 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'It’s close to you', text: '“It is not in heaven… nor across the sea. It is very close to you, in your mouth and in your heart, to do it.” What’s right isn’t out of reach.', source: 'Deuteronomy 30:11–14' },
      { title: 'You can always come back', text: '“You will return to the LORD your God… and the LORD will return to you.” <i>Teshuvah</i>, coming back, is always possible. This portion is read just before Rosh Hashanah.', source: 'Deuteronomy 30:1–3' },
      { title: 'Choose life', text: '“I have set before you life and death, blessing and curse. Choose life, so that you and your children may live.” Every day, choose what brings life.', source: 'Deuteronomy 30:19' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 29:10', headline: 'Some who stood in the covenant had joined by a trick.', text: 'On the day of his death, Rashi says, Moses gathers everyone into a covenant: leaders first, then elders, officers, every man, the children, the women, “and the stranger in your camp, from your woodcutter to your water-drawer.” Rashi, following the Midrash Tanchuma, says these were Canaanites who came to convert in Moses’ day by a ruse, as the Gibeonites would later fool Joshua; Moses made them cut wood and draw water. A ranked crowd, with motives no one could vouch for, and the verse says all of them stand “before the LORD your God.” Nobody waited for pure hearts.', try: 'Think of someone in your circle whose motives you doubt, the newcomer who wants something, the one who joined for the wrong reasons. Today treat them as fully part of it, the way the covenant did.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 29:12', headline: 'Rashi says the curses themselves are what keep Israel standing.', text: 'Ninety-eight curses have just been read. Rashi, from the Midrash Tanchuma, says the people’s faces went pale: who could survive this? So Moses calms them: “You are standing today, all of you.” You have angered God many times, and here you still are. Rashi hears a promise in “today”: like the day, which goes dark and then shines again, you have been made to shine and will shine again. Then a harder sentence: the curses and the sufferings are what keep you standing before Him. Survival here is not the absence of suffering, but what suffering leaves standing.', try: 'Write down the hardest stretch of your life so far and one thing you can do now only because you went through it. Then use it today for someone who is in the middle of their own.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 29:28', headline: 'Secret sins are God’s business; open ones are ours.', text: 'Moses warns of one person who secretly turns to idols and brings ruin on the land. Rashi voices the people’s protest: how can You punish us all for one man’s private thoughts? The verse answers: “The hidden things belong to the LORD our God, but what is revealed belongs to us and our children.” God deals with the secret sinner Himself; wrongs done in the open are ours to stop, and if we don’t, the whole community answers. Dots over “to us and our children,” Rashi adds, mark that this began only after they crossed the Jordan and swore together.', try: 'Name one wrong happening in plain sight in a group you belong to, at work, in your family, online, that everyone has agreed not to mention. Today say something to the person who can change it.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 30:6', headline: 'Ramban imagines a future when doing right no longer takes willpower.', text: 'Moses promises that after exile and return, “the LORD your God will circumcise your heart.” Back in chapter 10 he told them to do it themselves. The Ramban hears in it a Talmudic line (Shabbat 104a): whoever comes to purify himself is helped from above. Since creation, humans have been free to choose good or evil, and so to earn merit or guilt. In the days of the Messiah, choosing good will be natural; the heart will not crave what is wrong, as before Adam’s sin. Desire is the heart’s foreskin. Struggle is our condition now, but not our destiny.', try: 'Pick the one temptation you fight most often. Today take one practical step that puts it a little further out of reach, so it asks less of your willpower.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 30:9', headline: 'Sforno says a full return turns the sins themselves into merits.', text: '“The LORD will again delight in you for good, as He delighted in your fathers.” Sforno notices the comparison. This time the delight won’t come from the merit of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. It will be earned by a return “with all your heart and all your soul,” so complete that the sins themselves are counted as merits, and the penitent is cherished as the patriarchs were. In the Talmud (Yoma 86b) Reish Lakish makes the same claim and refines it: return out of fear turns deliberate sins into mere mistakes; return out of love turns them into merits.', try: 'Pick one wrong you regret but have never actually faced. Today make the whole return: admit it plainly, to the person or to God, and change one thing so it can’t happen the same way again.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 30:12', headline: 'The rabbis outvoted heaven, but couldn’t outvote one man’s tears.', text: '“It is not in heaven.” Rabbi Eliezer, alone against the sages over an oven’s purity, calls on a carob tree, a stream and finally a voice from heaven, and all side with him. Rabbi Yehoshua quotes this verse: the Torah is ours now; the majority rules. God, Elijah reports, smiles: “My children have defeated Me.” Then the sages ban Rabbi Eliezer; he weeps, and a third of the crops wither. When he finally pours out his hurt in prayer, their leader, Rabban Gamliel, dies. Heaven’s gates, says the Talmud (Bava Metzia 59b), never close to someone wounded by words.', try: 'Think of an argument you won, at work or at home, where the other person walked away humiliated. Today go back and repair things with the person, even if you keep the point.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 30:19', headline: 'God offers a free choice, then puts your hand on the answer.', text: '“I have set before you life and death, blessing and curse; choose life.” Heaven and earth are called as witnesses, and Rashi explains why: the sun has never once refused to rise, and the earth has never yielded barley from sown wheat, though they earn no reward. How much more should you, who do. Then on “choose life” he gives a parable. A father tells his son to pick any field in his estate, then sets the boy on the best one and says: “Choose this.” The freedom is real, but God is not neutral about how you use it.', try: 'Think of a choice you have been circling where you already know the life-giving answer. Stop weighing it today and take the first concrete step.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayeilech': {
    meaning: 'And he went',
    summary: 'On his 120th birthday, Moses tells the people he can no longer lead them and hands leadership to Joshua: “Be strong and courageous.” He writes down the Torah and commands that every seven years, at Sukkot, the whole people (men, women, children and strangers) gather to hear it read. God tells Moses to write a song that will speak to the people in future generations. It’s the shortest portion in the Torah.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Be strong and courageous', text: 'In front of everyone, Moses tells Joshua: <i>Chazak ve’ematz</i>, be strong and courageous. Passing the torch well means building up the next leader in public.', source: 'Deuteronomy 31:7' },
      { title: 'Teach it as a song', text: 'God calls the Torah’s teaching “this song.” Songs stay with us longer than lectures. Teach in ways people will remember.', source: 'Deuteronomy 31:19–21' },
      { title: 'Write your own Torah', text: '“Now write down this song.” The Talmud learns that every Jew should write a Torah scroll for themselves. The teaching has to become your own.', source: 'Deuteronomy 31:19 · Sanhedrin 21b' },
      { title: 'When God seems hidden', text: 'God warns that in hard times “I will hide My face.” Even then, the song will remind the people. When God feels far away, what we’ve learned keeps the connection.', source: 'Deuteronomy 31:17–18' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 31:2', headline: 'On his last day, Rashi says, Moses’ wellsprings of wisdom closed.', text: '“I am a hundred and twenty years old today; I can no longer go out and come in.” Today, Rashi says, is his birthday, and the day he dies. Rashi refuses the easy reading: Moses’ strength had not failed, for at his death “his eye was not dim.” “I can no longer” means “I am not permitted”: authority is passing to Joshua. Then, from the Talmud (Sotah 13b), worse: the traditions and wellsprings of wisdom were stopped up for him. On his final day the greatest teacher lacks full access to what he knew, and teaches anyway.', try: 'Name something you can no longer do the way you once could, a skill, a role, a closeness. Today use what you still have of it fully, for someone else, instead of mourning what’s gone.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 31:6', headline: 'Sforno names two abandonments: in the battle, and after it.', text: 'Moses, who will not cross the Jordan, tells the people who will: “Be strong and brave… the LORD your God goes with you; He will not fail you or forsake you.” Sforno hears two separate promises. He will not fail you: He will hold your hand in the hour of battle. He will not forsake you: afterward, too, He will not take His watchful eye off you. The second promise is the rarer one. People rally in a crisis; the loneliness usually comes later, when the emergency is over, the visitors have stopped coming, and everyone else has moved on.', try: 'Think of someone who went through a crisis a few months ago, a loss, a diagnosis, a breakup, when everyone showed up. Call them today, now that people have stopped.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 31:7', headline: 'Moses advised leading by consensus; God insisted on one leader.', text: 'Moses tells Joshua: “Be strong and brave, for you will come with this people into the land.” Rashi, from the Talmud (Sanhedrin 8a), hears a philosophy in that “with.” Moses meant: the elders will be with you; do everything by their opinion and advice. But later God tells Joshua, “you will bring the children of Israel”: even against their will. Everything depends on you, God says; if you must, take a stick and strike their heads. One leader for a generation, not two. The humblest of men would share the decision; God insists someone must own it.', try: 'Find one decision you have been waiting for others to agree on, in your family, at work, among friends. If it is truly yours to make, make it today and own the result.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 31:11', headline: 'A king wept at the Torah’s verdict; the crowd answered with flattery.', text: 'Every seven years, at Sukkot, the king reads from Deuteronomy to the whole people. The Mishnah (Sotah 7:8) remembers King Agrippa, of Herod’s line. He stood to read, and the sages praised him. When he reached “you may not set a foreigner over you,” tears ran from his eyes. The crowd cried: “Don’t be afraid, Agrippa, you are our brother, you are our brother!” A touching scene, until the Talmud (Sotah 41b) quotes Rabbi Natan: at that moment Israel deserved destruction, for flattering Agrippa. Comfort that rewrites the truth to spare a powerful man is not kindness.', try: 'Think of someone with power over you, a boss, a relative, a friend you need, whom you keep reassuring when they need the truth. Today tell them one true thing, kindly.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 31:18', headline: 'Ramban: noticing God’s absence is regret; it is not yet return.', text: 'God warns: the people will stray, and “I will hide My face.” Disaster comes; they say: “Isn’t it because our God is not among us that these evils have found us?” Yet the next verse repeats: “I will surely hide My face.” Why, after they admit it? The Ramban: that sentence is reflection and regret; they know they are guilty, but it is not yet a confession. The face of redemption stays hidden until confession and full return. The Talmud (Chagigah 5b) softens it: even hidden, Rava says, God speaks in dreams; Rav Yosef: His hand still shelters us.', try: 'Think of something that went wrong between you and someone, where you have told yourself “things just got hard.” Today name, to them or on paper, the specific thing you did.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 31:21', headline: 'The Torah will never be forgotten, Rabbi Shimon says, only scattered.', text: 'God tells Moses that this song “will not be forgotten from the mouths of their descendants.” Rashi calls it a promise: the Torah will never be wholly forgotten. The Talmud (Shabbat 138b) shows how much rode on it. When the sages gathered at Yavneh, they feared the Torah would be lost, as Amos warned: people will roam to find the word of God and not find it. Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai answered with this verse: Heaven forbid. “Not find it” means they won’t find a clear ruling and a clear teaching in one place. The Torah survives as an argument.', try: 'Pick a question in Jewish life you think is settled. Today read two opinions on it that disagree, and ask what each one is trying to protect.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 31:29', headline: 'As long as Joshua lived, Rashi says, Moses counted himself alive.', text: 'Moses ends grimly: “I know that after my death you will act corruptly.” Rashi objects: the Book of Judges says they served God all Joshua’s days. So “after my death” must mean after Joshua’s, and Rashi draws the lesson: a person’s student should be as dear to him as himself. As long as Joshua lived, it seemed to Moses that he was still alive. That day, Rashi notes, the silver trumpets only Moses could blow were hidden away, for “there is no rule on the day of death.” His power ends; his student is how he goes on.', try: 'Choose one person younger or newer than you and teach them, properly, one thing you know how to do. Give it a real hour today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Ha\'Azinu': {
    meaning: 'Listen',
    summary: 'Most of this portion is a poem, the Song of Moses. Calling heaven and earth as witnesses, Moses recalls how God cared for Israel like an eagle over its nest, how Israel grew comfortable and forgot, and how God will defend them in the end. Then God tells Moses to climb Mount Nebo and see the land from afar before he dies.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Ask the people who came before you', text: '“Remember the days of old… ask your father, and he will tell you; your elders, and they will explain.” Wisdom is handed down. Ask the people who came before you.', source: 'Deuteronomy 32:7' },
      { title: 'Teach like rain', text: '“May my teaching come down like rain, my words settle like dew.” Rain soaks in slowly and makes things grow. Teach gently, and let it sink in.', source: 'Deuteronomy 32:2' },
      { title: 'Comfort makes us forget', text: '“Jeshurun grew fat and kicked.” When life is easy, people forget who helped them. Having plenty is a test too.', source: 'Deuteronomy 32:15' },
      { title: 'Seeing it from afar', text: 'Moses climbs Mount Nebo to see the land he won’t enter. He sees the whole promise, even if he doesn’t get to walk in it. Sometimes our job is to begin what others will finish.', source: 'Deuteronomy 32:49–52' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 32:4', headline: 'Condemned to death, a rabbi and his wife divided this verse.', text: 'The Song makes its hardest claim early: “The Rock, His work is perfect; all His ways are just; a faithful God, without wrong.” The Talmud (Avodah Zarah 18a) puts it in the mouths of a family the Romans have condemned for teaching Torah. Rabbi Chanina ben Teradyon is sentenced to burn, his wife to the sword, his daughter to a brothel. Walking out, he says: “The Rock, His work is perfect.” His wife finishes: “a faithful God, without wrong.” His daughter adds a line from Jeremiah. Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi marvels at them. Jews still say these words at burials.', try: 'Think of a loss you have never made peace with. Don’t force an answer today; just write the one sentence of faith you can honestly say about it, and no more.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 32:11', headline: 'Rashi’s eagle carries its young on its wings, to take the arrow.', text: '“Like an eagle stirring its nest, hovering over its young, it spreads its wings and takes them.” Rashi, following the midrash, sees three kinds of care. The eagle doesn’t burst into the nest; it beats its wings among the branches so the chicks wake and gather strength. It hovers, touching and not touching. And when it moves them, it carries them not in its talons, like other birds, but on its wings. Other birds fear the eagle above; the eagle fears only the hunter’s arrow from below. Better it pierce me, the eagle says, than my children.', try: 'Think of someone you protect, a child, a parent, a friend in trouble. Today put yourself between them and one real danger or burden, even if it costs you something.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 32:18', headline: 'Moses calls God the one who went into labor with you.', text: '“You forgot the Rock who bore you, the God who writhed in labor with you.” Rashi reads <i>mecholelecha</i> as the one who brought you out of the womb, the word Psalms uses for a woman in childbirth. Near the end of the Torah, God is pictured as a mother in labor. Rashi then gives the rabbis’ reading of the rare word <i>teshi</i>: not “you forgot” but “you weakened.” Every time God came to do you good, you provoked Him and, as it were, sapped His power to do it. Ingratitude does not only wound the giver; it disables the gift.', try: 'Think of someone who keeps trying to do good for you, a parent, a partner, a friend, whom you make it hard for. Today let them help you, and thank them specifically.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 32:26', headline: 'Ramban admits that by strict justice, the exile would never end.', text: '“I would have said, I will scatter them and erase their memory from humankind, were it not for the enemy’s taunt.” The Ramban applies it to his own exile, unsparingly: by strict justice the Jews would stay scattered forever, and the merit of the patriarchs has run out. What saves them is God’s name. God made humans to know and thank Him, the Ramban says; when the world forgot, one people stayed devoted. If they vanished, His wonders would be forgotten, and the purpose of creating humanity would be lost. Israel’s survival is a testimony, not a reward.', try: 'Ask what your life testifies to, for someone watching who knows nothing else about Judaism. Do one thing today that would make that testimony truer.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 32:39', headline: 'The Talmud says the dead will rise still carrying their wounds.', text: '“I put to death and I bring to life; I wounded and I will heal.” Rava, in the Talmud (Pesachim 68a), asks why the second half is needed: if God can raise the dead, surely He can heal. The answer: both halves describe one person; the one who dies is the one brought back. Then a bolder reading: the dead will rise with the very injuries they died with, and only afterward be healed. Why? So no one can say, the Midrash explains, “These are not they, but others” (Bereshit Rabbah 95:1). God brings back you, not a stranger.', try: 'Think of a scar you usually hide, on your body or in your history. Today show it to one person you trust and tell them its story.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 32:43', headline: 'At the Song’s end, Rashi says, God appeases His own people.', text: 'The Song ends with vengeance on the enemies, and then two surprising lines. “Sing, O nations, of His people”: Rashi says the nations will praise Israel, “See how praiseworthy this people is, that they clung to God through all the troubles that passed over them and did not forsake Him.” Then: “He will atone His land, His people.” Rashi reads the verb as in Jacob’s hope to appease Esau. God will appease His land and His people for all the suffering they endured. After the people’s betrayals, the Song’s last line has God doing the appeasing.', try: 'Think of someone you were right to correct or keep at a distance, who suffered for it. Today, without taking back what was right, do something to comfort them.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 32:48', headline: 'Israel vowed to stop Moses from dying; God took him at noon.', text: '“That very day,” at high noon, Rashi says, God tells Moses to climb Mount Nebo and die. He links three of the times Scripture says it: Noah entering the ark, Israel leaving Egypt, and here; each time someone had sworn to stop it. The people said: the man who took us out of Egypt, split the sea, brought down the manna and gave us the Torah, we won’t let him go! God answers: I will bring him in at midday; whoever can prevent it, let him come and prevent it. Their love is real, and it is powerless.', try: 'Think of someone in your life who is aging or ill. Today spend real time with them, not because you can stop what is coming, but because you can’t.' },
    ],
  },

  'V\'Zot HaBerachah': {
    meaning: 'And this is the blessing',
    summary: 'The last portion of the Torah, read on Simchat Torah. Before he dies, Moses blesses each of the tribes, as Jacob once blessed his sons. Then he climbs Mount Nebo, God shows him the whole land, and Moses dies there. God buries him, and no one knows where. “There never again arose in Israel a prophet like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face.” Then the cycle begins again with Genesis.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'End with a blessing', text: 'Moses’ last act is to bless every tribe. After forty years of complaints and rebellions, he ends with love.', source: 'Deuteronomy 33:1' },
      { title: 'A blessing for every kind of life', text: 'Moses blesses each tribe differently: Levi to teach, Zebulun to trade, Issachar to study. Every kind of life gets its own blessing.', source: 'Deuteronomy 33:8–19' },
      { title: 'An unknown grave', text: '“No one knows his burial place to this day.” Commentators say it was so his grave wouldn’t become a shrine. Moses’ legacy is his teaching, not a monument.', source: 'Deuteronomy 34:6' },
      { title: 'End, and begin again', text: 'On Simchat Torah we finish Deuteronomy and right away start Genesis again. The Torah never really ends; we just start learning it again, a little wiser.', source: 'Deuteronomy 34:12 · Genesis 1:1' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 33:7', headline: 'Judah’s bones rolled in his coffin until Moses prayed for him.', text: 'Moses blesses Reuben, “Let Reuben live and not die,” then turns to Judah. Why Judah next? Rashi: because both confessed their sins. The Talmud (Sotah 7b) tells the rest. For all forty years in the desert, Judah’s bones rolled in his coffin, because he had once pledged himself to blame forever if Benjamin did not come home. Moses pleaded: who moved Reuben to confess? It was Judah, whom he saw confess first! “Hear, LORD, the voice of Judah,” and the bones settled into place. One honest admission taught another, and that merit undid a curse he had laid on himself.', try: 'Admit one thing today that you have been defending: to the person you wronged, plainly, without explanation. Someone watching you may find it easier to admit theirs.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 33:11', headline: 'Rashi says Moses saw the Maccabees coming and prayed for them.', text: 'For Levi, the priestly tribe, Moses prays: “Bless, LORD, his strength, and accept the work of his hands; strike the loins of those who rise against him.” Why would priests need help in battle? Rashi first aims it at those who would contest the priesthood. Then a second reading, from the Midrash: Moses foresaw the Hasmoneans, Mattathias and his sons, priests who would one day fight the Greeks. They would be a handful against tens of thousands, so Moses prayed for them. Over a thousand years ahead, he saw a few priests with swords and blessed their hands.', try: 'Find a small group fighting for something you believe in against long odds, a tiny congregation, a local cause, a new school. Today send them real help and tell them they are not alone.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 33:16', headline: 'The God of the thornbush is invoked over the brother they sold.', text: 'Blessing Joseph, Moses reaches back to his own beginning: “the favor of the One who dwelt in the thornbush.” Rashi: the God who first revealed Himself to me in the bush. Let it rest “on the head of the one set apart from his brothers”: Rashi says, the brother separated from them by being sold. Why the bush? On Exodus, Rashi explains that God spoke from a thornbush, not a finer tree, to say: “I am with him in distress.” The God who chose thorns is called down on the boy who was thrown in a pit.', try: 'Think of someone going through a thorny stretch right now. Today don’t send advice; go and be with them in it, in person if you can, for at least an hour.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 33:18', headline: 'Rashi puts the merchant ahead of the scholar he paid for.', text: '“Rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out, and Issachar, in your tents.” Issachar is the older brother, yet Zebulun is blessed first. Rashi explains: they made a partnership. Zebulun lived by the harbor and sailed to trade, and fed Issachar, who sat and studied Torah. Since Issachar’s learning came through Zebulun, Zebulun comes first. The Talmud (Megillah 6a) adds that Zebulun once complained: my brothers got fields and vineyards, and I got mountains and seas. God answered that everyone would need him, for the sea snail that gives the blue dye for tzitzit. The unglamorous work comes first.', try: 'Think of someone whose ordinary work makes your learning, prayer or creative work possible: a spouse, a parent, a donor, a custodian. Thank them today, and say exactly what their work made possible.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 33:25', headline: 'Moses, strong at a hundred and twenty, blesses an undrained old age.', text: '“Your bolts will be iron and bronze, and as your days, so your <i>dove</i>.” That last word appears nowhere else in Scripture. Rashi hears in it the root for draining away: the days of decline. As your best days, the days of your youth, so shall your old age be. The blessing comes from a man who has it: a few verses later the Torah says that at a hundred and twenty Moses’ eye was undimmed and his vigor had not fled. He doesn’t wish them more years, but later years as strong as the early ones.', try: 'Name one thing you did with joy in your best years that you have let drain away, a sport, an instrument, a friendship. Do it today, even badly.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 33:28', headline: 'Rashi separates the loneliness of fear from the solitude of safety.', text: 'Near the end: “Israel dwells in safety, alone.” Alone sounds bleak. Rashi reads it as peace: each family under its own vine and fig tree, spread apart, because once the enemy is gone no one needs to huddle together for protection. Then he distinguishes two kinds of alone. Not Jeremiah’s, “I sat alone because of Your hand, for You filled me with fury,” but the alone of Jacob’s blessing: “God will be with you and bring you back.” Being apart can be the mark of terror or of trust. The difference is whether you are alone with God.', try: 'Spend twenty minutes today genuinely alone, no phone and no noise. Notice whether it feels like Jeremiah’s solitude or Jacob’s, and ask what would change it.' },
      { verse: 'Deuteronomy 34:12', headline: 'The Torah’s last words, Rashi says, praise Moses for smashing the tablets.', text: 'The Torah’s last verse praises “all the mighty hand and the great awe that Moses did before the eyes of all Israel.” Which deed? Rashi, from the Sifrei: the moment his heart moved him to shatter the tablets before them, after the golden calf. And God agreed: He later speaks of “the tablets which you broke,” which the Talmud reads as <i>yishar kochacha</i>, well done for breaking them. The last word on the greatest prophet is not a miracle but the day he destroyed the holiest thing he held rather than hand it to people dancing around a calf.', try: 'Is there something precious, your time, your trust, your best work, that you keep handing to someone who can’t receive it? Today stop, even if stopping feels like breaking something.' },
    ],
  },
});
